WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Welcome to OmniView, episode 123 for July 25th, 2026.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.320
Multiple perspectives, one briefing.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.880
Here's the news, the perspectives, and where to look next.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.800
Saudi Arabia's strike on Yemen's main port

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.880
raises the risk of wider disruption to Red Sea shipping

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.120
that carries fuel and goods for millions

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.920
across the Middle East and beyond.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.580
Saudi Arabia carried out strikes on the port city

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:25.860
of Hodeidah in Yemen.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.920
The Saudi-led coalition stated the action

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.520
responded to attacks by Iranian-backed

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.280
Houthi rebels on Saudi shipping.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.480
The strikes follow a pattern of exchanges between the two sides.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.860
The coalition issued its statement early Saturday.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.500
Hodeidah functions as a major entry point for supplies

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.060
into Yemen.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.040
The Saudi-led coalition views the port

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.700
as a critical supply route for Houthi forces.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.540
It argues that hitting it is necessary

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.060
to protect commercial vessels in the Red Sea.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.940
Iranian officials and Houthi statements frame the strikes

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.940
as an escalation that endangers civilian infrastructure

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.280
and deepens foreign involvement in Yemen's civil war.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.740
Shipping companies and governments that rely on the route

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.260
have noted potential delays in fuel and container traffic.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.860
The scale of any immediate effect remains unclear

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.040
from initial reports.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.700
Regional governments continue to monitor

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.600
whether additional strikes will follow in the coming days.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.880
AP News details the coalition's statement

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.560
and Houthi attacks on shipping.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.500
Shifting now to protests in India,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.680
thousands of young people have joined street demonstrations

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.160
across India while also posting memes,

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.560
parody videos, and jokes targeting Prime Minister

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.700
Narendra Modi on social media.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.700
The protests combine traditional rallies

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.140
with online satire.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.900
The content has spread rapidly through digital platforms.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:54.800
Organizers describe the humor as a way to reach younger voters

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.600
and keep pressure on the government

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.380
without immediate confrontation.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.020
Government supporters see the approach

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.640
as disrespectful to national institutions

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.280
and argue it distracts from policy debates.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.460
Observer's note that both the street actions

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.180
and the online content have spread rapidly.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.960
Their long-term effect on political support

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.120
is not yet measured.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.540
The combination of physical gatherings

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.040
and digital posts allows the demonstrations

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.660
to reach audiences inside and outside major cities.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.960
AP News reports on the combination of rallies

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.560
and social media content.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.580
Next, a change at the International Criminal Court.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.500
The International Criminal Court has removed

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.300
Kareem Khan from his post as chief prosecutor

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.740
following allegations of sexual misconduct.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.640
The court announced the decision without detailing

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.440
further steps at this stage.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.480
The removal centers on claims of misconduct

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.660
raised internally.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.100
Court officials emphasize that the removal

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.120
reflects the institution's commitment

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.500
to internal standards and accountability for its own staff.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.980
Some governments and legal groups

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.540
that have worked with the ICC

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.560
welcome the move as necessary

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.260
to maintain credibility in ongoing cases.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.180
Others, including voices from countries

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.820
under ICC scrutiny,

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.940
question whether the process was transparent enough

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.360
and whether it affects pending investigations.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.080
The decision leaves the court's prosecutorial office

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.980
without its previous leadership while cases continue.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.160
BBC News covers the removal announcement

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.040
and allegations.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.960
Meanwhile, wildfires continue near Madrid.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.440
Wildfires burning near Madrid

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.480
have been described by a senior official

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.320
as impossible to control under current conditions.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.100
High winds are expected to complicate

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.280
firefighting efforts in the coming days.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.280
The fires have prompted assessments of available resources.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.060
Spanish authorities have focused on evacuation orders

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.220
and resource allocation to protect populated areas.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.100
Local residents and regional governments

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.360
stress the immediate need for more equipment and personnel.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.240
Meteorological reports highlight the role

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.220
of dry conditions and wind in the fires spread.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.440
Forecast indicate little short-term relief.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.920
Officials continue to track how shifting wind patterns

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.020
may affect containment lines around the capital region.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:19.660
BBC News provides live updates on the Madrid area fires.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:23.020
Reuters notes wind forecasts and official assessments.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.820
In technology developments,

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.800
NVIDIA and South Korea's SK Group

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.160
have announced a joint plan for AI data centers valued

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.920
at more than $500 billion.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.620
The initiative includes a memory chip partnership.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.580
It covers construction and technology supply

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.920
over multiple years.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.600
The partnership focuses on memory components needed

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.600
for large-scale AI systems.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.120
The companies present the project as a response

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.060
to growing global demand for AI infrastructure.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.080
Industry analysts note that SK's memory expertise

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.460
complements NVIDIA's chip technology,

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.000
potentially speeding up supply chains.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.780
Governments in both countries have signaled support

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.280
for such large-scale technology investments

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.720
as part of broader industrial strategies.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.440
The multi-year timeline allows for phase development

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.640
of facilities and component production.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.240
Reuters outlines the scale and partnership details.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.780
Now some progress worth knowing about.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.200
Lebanon continues to produce educated workers,

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.940
yet chronic political and economic problems

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.620
have driven many to emigrate.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.940
Recent discussions have focused on creating conditions

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.480
that would allow more of them to build careers inside the country.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.560
The country's universities and training programs

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.960
continue to generate skilled graduates.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.340
Lebanese institutions and diaspora networks

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.080
point to the country's universities

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.580
and professional training as sources of talent

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.500
that could support domestic recovery

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:51.860
if governance improves.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.880
International organizations have noted

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:56.920
that reducing emigration would require

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:00.160
steady progress on basic services and security.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.300
The main complication remains the slow pace

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.480
of political reforms needed to retain that talent.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:08.760
Efforts to address these issues

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.040
center on improving everyday governance

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.660
and economic stability.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.540
The economist examines the link between

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.680
governance and retaining skilled workers.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.360
Now to really understand this story,

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.500
there's something most coverage leaves out.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.280
Most coverage of an ICC prosecutor's removal

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.600
treats it as a simple personnel decision.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.340
Yet the court structure gives the assembly

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:34.100
of state's parties the body of member countries,

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:36.660
the formal power to remove the prosecutor

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.700
through a two-thirds vote after an independent review.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.000
The process begins with an internal investigation

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.240
by the court's disciplinary mechanisms.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.640
It then moves to the assembly for a final decision.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:50.780
This creates multiple layers

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:53.340
that aim to balance independence with oversight.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.360
This setup was written into the Rome Statute

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:58.080
to prevent any single government

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.220
from easily sidelineing a prosecutor.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.980
It also means removals can take months

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.360
and require broad diplomatic agreement.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.280
The Rome Statute established these steps

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.020
in 1998 when the court was created.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.060
It drew on lessons from earlier tribunals

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.040
where political pressure had interfered

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:16.940
with prosecutions.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.200
Knowing this, when reports describe a removal

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.100
as sudden or politically driven,

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.640
readers can check whether the assembly vote

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:26.960
and the underlying investigative report

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.160
have been made public.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.540
The assembly's role ensures that no one country can act alone.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.000
This protects the court's independence

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.860
while still allowing accountability

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:39.140
when evidence of misconduct emerges.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.340
The concrete detail to verify next time

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:44.200
is whether the assembly has published

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.700
the timeline and vote tally for the removal.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.800
Checking the official record also reveals

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.300
how many states participated in the decision

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.200
and whether any formal objections

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:55.980
were recorded during the process.

